[P2] Use asymmetric float32 intervals for median feasibility
context_reference_audit/audit.py:145
The symmetric `np.spacing(p)/2` bound overstates downward rounding uncertainty at powers of two, so an impossible saved median can still pass the new gap check. For example, a float32 row beginning `[0.25, 0.25, 2e-8, 0.25, float32(0.25-2e-8)]` admits normalized float64 probabilities, but their cumulative mass through index 2 must exceed 0.500000005: each archived 0.25 can decrease by only about 7.45e-9. A corrupted median at index 3 nevertheless passes because this calculation allows each 0.25 to decrease by about 1.49e-8, and index 3 has a positive crossing increment. Compute cumulative lower and upper bounds from `probability_rounding_intervals`, which already handles the unequal predecessor/successor spacing, and add a regression case at a power-of-two boundary.

